editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:207:It did not take long, because he did not own much: clothes, three notebooks, and a handful of small tools he had made himself in the workshop, more for remembering than for using. Among them were a file with a handle he had turned on Hesk's lathe at ten and a pair of fine tweezers ground from spring steel that closed a hair off true. He packed them anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:209:He held each notebook before he put it in the bag. The first was three years old, its spine cracked and its cover gone soft. It was full of a younger boy's observations so earnest that he could not be embarrassed by them: *Garrik says a bearing is happiest when it is doing nothing. The grey dog does not like the colour blue.* The second covered the year he had first seen how far apart the Outer District and the Inner one really were. The five pages about the wall had felt like an essay when he wrote them and felt now more like a warning he had written to himself without knowing who it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:The third was the current one, with eleven descriptions of Kindling and two stars and a Stone Path declaration in Joren's round hand near the back. Folded into its back pages was a copy of a registry entry about four people he had never met and was never going to stop thinking about. Into the bag they went in order, the oldest underneath, as you stack a thing you mean to keep adding to.
